    "question": "Would the auditor’s conclusion change if the advance, which has \nremained overdue for more than 18 months, was guaranteed by a State \nGovernment instead of the Central Government?",
    "caseTitle": "Case Scenario 25: Standards on Auditing & Ethical Standards",
    "scenarioText": "Verma & Co. has been appointed as the central statutory auditor of Quick Trust \nBank Ltd., a private sector bank registered with RBI, for F.Y. 202 3–24. \nMr. Verma, the engagement partner, while examining the computation of DTL \n(Demand and Time Liabilities), raised his suspicion regarding the inclusion and \nexclusion of the following items: \nSr. No. Particulars of Item Inclusion/Exclusion \n1 Amounts received in INR for disposal of import \nbills shown under Sundry Deposits \nInclusion \n2 Net credit balance of Branch Adjustment \nAccount \nInclusion \n3 Amount received from ECGC by invoking the \nguarantee \nExclusion \n4 Margins held and kept in sundry deposits for \nfunded facilities. \nInclusion \n5 Unaccounted inward remittances received on \nbehalf of customers \nInclusion \nDuring the course of audit, Verma & Co. also observed that one large advance \naccount of the bank had no recovery of principal or interest for the past 18 \nmonths. The bank has not applied the NPA norms as well as income recognition \nnorms to this particular account . When queried the bank management replied \nthat this account was guaranteed by the central government and hence these \nnorms were not applicable. The bank has not invoked the guarantee. \nAdditionally, Verma & Co. was offered Stock audit of a sister concern of Quick \nTrust Bank Ltd. for F.Y. 202 3–24, and Branch audit of its Andheri Branch for F.Y. \n2023–24 and Bandra Branch for F.Y. 2024–25 , respectively. \nBased on the above facts, answer the following MCQs:",
    "options": [
      {
        "id": "A",
        "text": "Yes, since the special exemption from NPA classification is not \navailable for State Government guaranteed advances, and the \naccount would be treated as NPA after 90 days of overdue."
      },
      {
        "id": "B",
        "text": "Yes, but only income recognition norms would change, while asset \nclassification would remain unaffected."
      },
      {
        "id": "C",
        "text": "No, both Central and State Government guarantees are treated \nidentically for asset classification and income recognition under RBI \nIRAC norms."
      },
      {
        "id": "D",
        "text": "No, the account would be classified as NPA only on invocation and \nrepudiation of the State Government guarantee."
      }
    ],
    "correctAnswer": "A",
    "explanation": "Under Standards on Auditing & Ethical Standards, as per the official ICAI Case Scenario Booklet guidance.",
    "reference": "ICAI May 2026 Booklet Case Scenario 25; Standards on Auditing & Ethical Standards"
